-## Subtopics
+**Parameters:**
+*   **Density ($\tau$):** Controls fog thickness. Constant for uniform fog [S2, unofficial].
+*   **Scattering Coefficient ($a$):** Probability of photon scattering [S2, unofficial].
+*   **Step Size ($\Delta l$):** Distance between samples. Smaller values reduce artifacts but increase cost [S2, unofficial].
+*   **Phase Function ($P$):** Schlick approximation of Henyey-Greenstein is used for real-time performance [S2, unofficial].
 
-### Procedural Volumetric Cloud Rendering
-Renders volumetric clouds using procedural noise functions and ray-marching. Chosen when clouds are the primary volumetric element and do not require interaction with scene geometry shadows in the same way as fog. [Link](3480-procedural-volumetric-cloud-rendering.md)
-
-### Mr-Voxel Volume Rendering
-Uses voxel grids for volume data. Chosen for complex, non-uniform volumes where spatial acceleration structures are beneficial. [Link](3874-mr-voxel-volume-rendering.md)
-
-### DVGO Density and Color Rendering
-Uses Dual-Volume Grid Optimization for efficient density and color storage. Chosen for high-quality neural or grid-based volume rendering. [Link](12839-dvgo-density-and-color-rendering.md)
+**Edge Cases and Pitfalls:**
+*   **Slice Artifacts:** Regular sampling grids cause visible banding ("slices") because rays sample aligned points. Fixed by jittering [S2, unofficial].
+*   **Noise:** Jittering introduces noise, especially with large step sizes [S2, unofficial].
+*   **Infinite Marching:** Rays missing the scene must be clamped to $z_{far}$ [S2, unofficial].
 
 ## Operating characteristics
-*   **Time Complexity:** $O(N \cdot P)$, where $N$ is the number of pixels and $P$ is the number of steps per ray. Performance is heavily dependent on step size and early-out efficiency [S2, unofficial].
-*   **Memory Complexity:** $O(1)$ per pixel for the marching loop, plus storage for depth buffer and light data [S2, unofficial].
-*   **Measured Performance:** Not given in the sources.
-*   **Strengths:** Simple to implement, allows for early-out optimization, works well with standard depth buffers, and can simulate dynamic lighting interactions (soft shadows, god rays) [S2, unofficial].
-*   **Limitations:** Slice artifacts if not jittered, noise with large step sizes, and performance degradation with high step counts or large volumes [S2, unofficial].
-*   **When to use:** Real-time applications requiring simple, uniform fog or simple participating media effects where performance is critical and high-fidelity non-uniform volumes are not required [S2, unofficial].
-*   **When not to use:** When complex, non-uniform volumes with detailed internal structure are needed, or when backward ray-marching is required for specific lighting effects [S2, unofficial].
+*   **Time Complexity:** $O(N \cdot M)$, where $N$ is the number of pixels and $M$ is the number of steps per ray.
+*   **Memory Complexity:** $O(1)$ per pixel (excluding depth buffer and shadow maps).
+*   **Strengths:** Simple implementation; allows early-out optimization; works well for uniform or simple procedural fog [S2, unofficial].
+*   **Limitations:** High computational cost for large volumes or small step sizes; prone to noise without temporal or spatial filtering; slice artifacts require jittering [S2, unofficial].
+*   **When to Use:** Real-time applications requiring simple aerial perspective or light shafts with limited budget [S2, unofficial].
+*   **When Not to Use:** Complex, non-uniform volumes requiring high accuracy; scenes with many dynamic light sources requiring shadow maps for every sample [S2, unofficial].
 
 ## Background
-Ray-marched volumetric fog is a technique used in real-time graphics to simulate participating media. It is based on the principles of volume rendering, where the integral of radiance along a ray is approximated via discretization [S2, unofficial]. The specific implementation described here follows the approach of Toth and Umenhofer (TU09) for forward ray-marching, adapted for real-time full-screen shaders [S2, unofficial]. It is used in games like Horizon Zero Dawn, Death Stranding, and Returnal to simulate dynamic fog and light shafts [S2, unofficial]. The term "Fog Filter" is also used in older volume rendering software like DF3 Viewer [S1].
+Volumetric fog is a medium that emits or absorbs light, used for aerial perspective [S1]. The technique of ray-marching fog in games, such as in *Horizon Zero Dawn*, *Death Stranding*, and *Returnal*, treats fog as participating media traced via ray-marching [S2, unofficial]. The specific forward ray-marching implementation described here follows the approach of Toth and Umenhofer (Real-time Volumetric Lighting in Participating Media) but adapts it for forward marching to enable early-out optimizations [S2, unofficial].
 
